  "Number": 11,
  "Question": "The expression \\(1-\\dfrac1{1+\\sqrt3}+\\dfrac1{1-\\sqrt3}\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1-\\sqrt3\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(-\\sqrt3\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\sqrt3\\)",
    "E": "\\(1+\\sqrt3\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The denominators \\(1+\\sqrt3\\) and \\(1-\\sqrt3\\) are both nonzero. They are conjugates, so their product simplifies by the difference-of-squares formula:\n\\[\n(1-\\sqrt3)(1+\\sqrt3)=1-3=-2.\n\\]\nCombine the fractional terms first. Their common-denominator numerator is\n\\[\n(1+\\sqrt3)-(1-\\sqrt3)=2\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\frac1{1-\\sqrt3}-\\frac1{1+\\sqrt3}\n=\\frac{2\\sqrt3}{-2}=-\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nAdding the initial \\(1\\) gives \\(1-\\sqrt3\\), choice A.",
